From: Ludovic Zammit <luzammi@akamai.com>
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] dal/node: eliminate SELECT NOW() roundtrips during node insert + last_seen

A MariaDB general-log capture of a single MAC Access-Request showed three
"SELECT NOW()" queries per auth that exist purely to populate timestamp
columns on subsequent INSERT/UPDATE statements -- 1.5 wasted network
roundtrips each, all to fetch a value MariaDB could resolve inline.

Two of those came from pf::dal::node:

  - _insert_data sets $data->{detect_date} = $self->now  (pf::dal->now
    runs SELECT NOW() and returns the string).
  - update_last_seen does $self->last_seen($self->now()) for the same
    reason, then relies on a later save to flush.

Both can be replaced with a scalar-ref literal "NOW()", which SQL::Abstract
emits as raw SQL at INSERT/UPDATE time. pf::node::node_update_last_seen
(node.pm:1225) already uses exactly this pattern, so there's an in-tree
precedent.

Behavior change:
  * After this patch, $node_obj->{detect_date} is not populated on the
    Perl-side object during the INSERT codepath (the value is computed
    by the server). Callers that re-read the object after save get the
    value back from the next SELECT, which is unchanged.
  * update_last_seen now issues an UPDATE directly via update_items
    instead of going through the setter. Object-side $self->{last_seen}
    is no longer mutated. Callers that need the value can re-read.
  * pf::dal->now() itself is NOT changed; other callers use it as a
    bind parameter inside DATE_SUB(?, INTERVAL ? SECOND) and would break
    if it returned a scalar-ref.

Measured impact: ~2 SELECT NOW() roundtrips eliminated per Access-Request
(one for detect_date on first-time MACs, one for last_seen on every auth).
On the test box this is a small fraction of mariadbd CPU, but every
roundtrip removed adds headroom.

Apply from /usr/local/pf/:

    patch -p1 < 0003-dal-node-eliminate-select-now-roundtrips.patch

Rebuild containers that ship lib/ (e.g. pfperl-api) and restart any
host-side Perl workers (packetfence-pfqueue-backend) so they reload modules.

---
 lib/pf/dal/node.pm | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/pf/dal/node.pm b/lib/pf/dal/node.pm
--- a/lib/pf/dal/node.pm
+++ b/lib/pf/dal/node.pm
@@ -181,7 +181,10 @@ sub _insert_data {
         return $status, $data;
     }
     if ($data->{detect_date} eq '0000-00-00 00:00:00') {
-       $data->{detect_date} = $self->now;
+       # Use a literal SQL expression so the timestamp resolves on the server during the
+       # INSERT, rather than doing a separate SELECT NOW() roundtrip first. $data is consumed
+       # locally by SQL::Abstract which treats scalar-refs as raw SQL.
+       $data->{detect_date} = \"NOW()";
     }

     $data->{mac} = clean_mac($data->{mac});
@@ -197,8 +200,16 @@ update_last_seen

 sub update_last_seen {
     my ($self) = @_;
-    $self->last_seen($self->now());
-    return ;
+    # Issue a single UPDATE with literal NOW() instead of (a) SELECT NOW() to fetch a string,
+    # (b) storing it on the object via the setter, (c) waiting for a later save to flush. This
+    # mirrors pf::node::node_update_last_seen at node.pm:1225 which already uses this pattern.
+    my $mac = $self->{mac};
+    return unless defined $mac && length $mac;
+    my ($status, $rows) = pf::dal::node->update_items(
+        -set   => { last_seen => \['NOW()'] },
+        -where => { mac => $mac },
+    );
+    return;
 }

 =head2 _load_locationlog
